 "music": "bed",
 "title": "Docker vs VM",
 "sources": [
  "Docker docs, 'What is a container?' (official, fetched 2026-11-17): a container is an isolated process with all the files it needs to run; a VM is an entire operating system with its own kernel, hardware drivers, programs, and applications; running a VM to isolate one application adds a lot of overhead; when you run multiple containers they all share the same kernel, so you can fit more applications on less infrastructure; containers are self-contained and do not depend on software preinstalled on the host. https://docs.docker.com/get-started/docker-concepts/the-basics/what-is-a-container/",
  "NIST SP 800-190, Application Container Security Guide (fetched 2026-11-17): section 2.2, with containers multiple apps share the same OS kernel instance but are segregated from each other, and the separation is far less than that provided to VMs by a hypervisor; section 3.5.2, a shared kernel invariably results in a larger inter-object attack surface and the isolation provided by container runtimes is not as high as that provided by hypervisors; section 3.5.3, flaws in low-level host components such as kernel primitives can impact all the containers and apps that run on the host. https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/SpecialPublications/NIST.SP.800-190.pdf"
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "A virtual machine takes a while to boot, but a container starts in moments. Why?",
   "caps": "VM *BOOTS SLOWLY | CONTAINER *STARTS FAST | *WHY?"
  },
  {
   "text": "A virtual machine is an entire operating system, with its own kernel, drivers, and applications.",
   "caps": "A VM IS A *WHOLE OS | OWN *KERNEL | OWN *DRIVERS"
  },
  {
   "text": "A container is just an isolated process, with all the files it needs to run.",
   "caps": "A CONTAINER | IS AN *ISOLATED PROCESS | PLUS ITS *FILES"
  },
  {
   "text": "Containers share the host's kernel, so there's no second operating system to boot.",
   "caps": "*SHARED KERNEL | NO *SECOND OS | NOTHING TO *BOOT"
  },
  {
   "text": "That's why you can fit many more of them on the same machine.",
   "caps": "*MORE CONTAINERS | SAME *MACHINE"
  },
  {
   "text": "And because they're self-contained, a container doesn't depend on anything installed on the host.",
   "caps": "*SELF-CONTAINED | NOTHING *INSTALLED ON HOST"
  },
  {
   "text": "But here's the catch: they all share one kernel, so a kernel bug can affect every container, and isolation is weaker than a VM's.",
   "caps": "BUT THERE IS A *CATCH | ONE *SHARED KERNEL | *WEAKER ISOLATION"
  },
  {
   "text": "So use containers for packaging and speed, and reach for a VM when you need hard isolation.",
   "caps": "CONTAINERS: *PACKAGING + SPEED | VMS: *HARD ISOLATION"
  }
 ],
